{
  "generated_text": "To solve the problem, we start by letting the two solutions of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + 48 = 0\\) be \\(3k\\) and \\(k\\) for some constant \\(k\\). According to Vieta's formulas, the sum of the roots of the quadratic equation \\(x^2 + bx + 48 = 0\\) is equal to \\(-b\\) and the product of the roots is equal to 48. Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\n3k + k = -b \\implies 4k = -b \\implies b = -4k\n\\]\n\n\\[\n(3k)(k) = 48 \\implies 3k^2 = 48 \\implies k^2 = 16 \\implies k = 4 \\text{ or } k = -4\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the corresponding values of \\(b\\) for both \\(k = 4\\) and \\(k = -4\\):\n\n1. If \\(k = 4\\), then \\(b = -4k = -4 \\cdot 4 = -16\\).\n2. If \\(k = -4\\), then \\(b = -4k = -4 \\cdot (-4) = 16\\).\n\nThe largest possible value of \\(b\\) is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
